   "post_data": {
    "post_id": 2024470,
    "post_type": "forum",
    "topic_id": 367745,
    "poster_id": 67223,
    "post_rendered": "Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\"  width=\"12\" height=\"12\" > be a convex quadrilateral <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/e/f9efaf9474c5658c4089523e2aff4e11488f8603.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABCD$\"  width=\"57\" height=\"13\" > and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/1/d/51da37d984564162c87710ca27bea422f657fb73.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$O$\"  width=\"13\" height=\"12\" > a point inside it. The feet of the perpendiculars from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/1/d/51da37d984564162c87710ca27bea422f657fb73.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$O$\"  width=\"13\" height=\"12\" > to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/5/9/15991b3141a86264dede361a307e9d9e1753d4af.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB, BC, CD, DA$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"139\" height=\"16\" > are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/2/1/421ea683284b07cc38c25b9b0e30ba4b75e51988.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_1, B_1, C_1, D_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"107\" height=\"16\" > respectively. The feet of the perpendiculars from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/1/d/51da37d984564162c87710ca27bea422f657fb73.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$O$\"  width=\"13\" height=\"12\" > to the sides of <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/0/9e0ec34cd8860ceb97113f81ba768ba739ad5166.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S_i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"15\" height=\"15\" >,</span> the quadrilateral <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/4/7/947e8104faed1fb480152e5608acaef03db814b8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_iB_iC_iD_i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"76\" height=\"15\" >,</span> are <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/2/3/823d82a116e81b5317b619a4bc3ee5ac60ad2619.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_{i+1}B_{i+1}C_{i+1}D_{i+1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"142\" height=\"17\" >,</span> where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/d/2fd50edf36e99cd5f48e1dd56caf9d1c44f1f624.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$i = 1, 2, 3.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"77\" height=\"16\" > Prove that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/2/7/927ca6f264730b7f22ea5e06ee7ac6872819f94e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S_4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"17\" height=\"15\" > is similar to S.",
    "post_canonical": "Let $S$ be a convex quadrilateral $ABCD$ and $O$ a point inside it. The feet of the perpendiculars from $O$ to $AB, BC, CD, DA$ are $A_1, B_1, C_1, D_1$ respectively. The feet of the perpendiculars from $O$ to the sides of $S_i$, the quadrilateral $A_iB_iC_iD_i$, are $A_{i+1}B_{i+1}C_{i+1}D_{i+1}$, where $i = 1, 2, 3.$ Prove that $S_4$ is similar to S.",
    "username": "Amir Hossein",
