   "post_data": {
    "post_id": 2024475,
    "post_type": "forum",
    "topic_id": 367749,
    "poster_id": 67223,
    "post_rendered": "Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\"  width=\"14\" height=\"12\" > be a set of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\"  width=\"9\" height=\"12\" > sequences each having <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\"  width=\"10\" height=\"8\" > terms equal to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"8\" height=\"12\" > or <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/a/e/bae5aba07d37ff6ff813107e76260fb31ad5794e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"22\" height=\"12\" >.</span> The product of two such sequences <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/f/2/8f22ebc14538f611c6db4405a055a8a3d2cbbf76.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(a_1, a_2, \\ldots , a_n)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"114\" height=\"18\" > and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/8/b1841ee531e777fb8d29e122ea403c6274a4e266.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(b_1, b_2, \\ldots , b_n)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"108\" height=\"18\" >  is defined as <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/c/9/3c93e6777133832c2f17b45b183d96cceedc64a0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(a_1b_1, a_2b_2, \\ldots , a_nb_n)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"161\" height=\"18\" >.</span> Prove that there exists a sequence <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/7/0/970fcc72133511adc1cf71222274b41251ffdb97.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(c_1, c_2, \\ldots , c_n)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"108\" height=\"18\" >  such that the intersection of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\"  width=\"14\" height=\"12\" > and the set containing all sequences from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\"  width=\"14\" height=\"12\" > multiplied by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/7/0/970fcc72133511adc1cf71222274b41251ffdb97.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(c_1, c_2, \\ldots , c_n)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"108\" height=\"18\" > contains at most <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/1/4/7146a88ddbf3189314f563bb317f48a699f1587b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{k^2}{2^n}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"20\" height=\"39\" > sequences.",
    "post_canonical": "Let $B$ be a set of $k$ sequences each having $n$ terms equal to $1$ or $-1$. The product of two such sequences $(a_1, a_2, \\ldots , a_n)$ and $(b_1, b_2, \\ldots , b_n)$  is defined as $(a_1b_1, a_2b_2, \\ldots , a_nb_n)$. Prove that there exists a sequence $(c_1, c_2, \\ldots , c_n)$  such that the intersection of $B$ and the set containing all sequences from $B$ multiplied by $(c_1, c_2, \\ldots , c_n)$ contains at most $\\frac{k^2}{2^n}$ sequences.",
    "username": "Amir Hossein",
    "post_number": 1,
    "post_format": "bbcode",
    "category_name": "High School Olympiads",
    "category_id": 6,
    "poster_avatar": "//avatar.artofproblemsolving.com/avatar_67223.png?t=1769913889"
