  {
   "item_id": 2024462,
   "item_text": "6",
   "item_forum_id": 0,
   "item_type": "post",
   "in_feed": null,
   "is_watched": null,
   "item_score": "993",
   "id": "29144",
   "item_level": "993",
   "post_data": {
    "post_id": 2024462,
    "post_type": "forum",
    "topic_id": 367740,
    "poster_id": 67223,
    "post_rendered": "A lattice point in the plane is a point both of whose coordinates are integers. Each lattice point has four neighboring points: upper, lower, left, and right. Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\"  width=\"9\" height=\"12\" > be a circle with radius <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/2/d/d2d0ac77141511e2109a7ad6286e75a178754b40.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r \\geq 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"41\" height=\"14\" >,</span> that does not pass through any lattice point. An interior boundary point is a lattice point lying inside the circle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\"  width=\"9\" height=\"12\" > that has a neighboring point lying outside <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\"  width=\"9\" height=\"12\" >.</span> Similarly, an exterior boundary point is a lattice point lying outside the circle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\"  width=\"9\" height=\"12\" > that has a neighboring point lying inside <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\"  width=\"9\" height=\"12\" >.</span> Prove that there are four more exterior boundary points than interior boundary points.",
    "post_canonical": "A lattice point in the plane is a point both of whose coordinates are integers. Each lattice point has four neighboring points: upper, lower, left, and right. Let $k$ be a circle with radius $r \\geq 2$, that does not pass through any lattice point. An interior boundary point is a lattice point lying inside the circle $k$ that has a neighboring point lying outside $k$. Similarly, an exterior boundary point is a lattice point lying outside the circle $k$ that has a neighboring point lying inside $k$. Prove that there are four more exterior boundary points than interior boundary points.",
    "username": "Amir Hossein",
    "post_number": 1,
    "post_format": "bbcode",
    "category_name": "High School Olympiads",
    "category_id": 6,
    "poster_avatar": "//avatar.artofproblemsolving.com/avatar_67223.png?t=1769913889"
   }
  }
